If your company has grown over the last few years, you may have wondered
whether it still qualifies as an SME, and which rules decide that. The SME
definition in Malaysia matters more than most owners realise: it can affect
which support programmes you can join and how you're treated by agencies and
lenders. And it's about to be reviewed for the first time in over a decade.

Here's what the current definition says, what the government has announced
about changing it, and what a growing business should do while the review
runs.

## What's changing: the SCALE Action Plan for 2027

In late October, Entrepreneur and Cooperatives Development Minister Steven Sim
unveiled the SCALE Action Plan for 2027 at the SAMENTA SME CEO Forum. It has
five priorities: SME Redefinition, Cash Flow Protection, AI for MSMEs, Local
Content Requirement and Easing MSME Financing
([Bernama](https://www.bernama.com/en/general/news.php?id=2624266),
[The Star](https://www.thestar.com.my/news/nation/2026/10/24/sim-unveils-scale-action-plan-for-2027)).

The first priority is the one this post is about. The minister said the
current SME definition, which dates back to 2013, needs to be reviewed to
reflect changes in the business environment, including AI and new business
models. The ministry plans to complete the review by 2027 and is considering
legislation, regulation or policy as the route
([Bernama](https://www.bernama.com/en/general/news.php?id=2624266)).

Two other things happened this month that point the same way:

- **Growth-stage support.** On 5 November, Deputy Prime Minister Fadillah
  Yusof said the government is open to a stronger, more structured support
  framework for SMEs that have moved past their initial growth phase
  ([Malay Mail](https://www.malaymail.com/news/malaysia/2026/11/05/govt-open-to-stronger-support-framework-for-smes-moving-beyond-initial-growth-says-fadillah/242051)).
  No framework or timeline has been announced yet.
- **Financing quality.** At the launch of the World Bank's latest Malaysia
  Economic Monitor on 11 November, Economy Minister Akmal Nasrullah Mohd Nasir
  cited its finding that SMEs fund about two-thirds of their fixed-asset
  investment from their own resources, and that bank financing covers around
  28% of SME fixed investment compared with about 50% for large frontier firms
  ([Malay Mail](https://www.malaymail.com/news/malaysia/2026/11/11/funding-malaysias-smes-is-about-quality-and-not-quantity-says-economy-minister/242866)).

Put together, the policy focus is shifting from how many SMEs Malaysia has to
how well its better SMEs can grow.

## The current SME definition in Malaysia

Until the review concludes, SME Corporation Malaysia's existing definition
still applies. A business is an SME if it meets **either** the sales turnover
test **or** the full-time employee test for its sector
([SME Corp](https://smecorp.gov.my/index.php/en/policies/2020-02-11-08-01-24/sme-definition)):

| Size | Manufacturing | Services and other sectors |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Micro | Turnover under RM300,000 or fewer than 5 employees | Turnover under RM300,000 or fewer than 5 employees |
| Small | RM300,000 to under RM15 million, or 5 to under 75 employees | RM300,000 to under RM3 million, or 5 to under 30 employees |
| Medium | RM15 million to RM50 million, or 75 to 200 employees | RM3 million to RM20 million, or 30 to 75 employees |

Where a business lands in different categories on the two tests, SME Corp
says the lower category applies. The full rules are in its
[Guideline on SME Definition](https://smecorp.gov.my/images/pdf/2024/Guideline_on_SME_Definition_March_2024_Final.pdf).

### Who is excluded

Size isn't the only test. SME Corp requires an SME to be registered with SSM
or an equivalent body, and excludes companies listed on the Bursa main board
and subsidiaries of main-board listed companies, multinationals,
government-linked companies, MKD companies and state-owned enterprises
([SME Corp](https://smecorp.gov.my/index.php/en/policies/2020-02-11-08-01-24/sme-definition)).
A small subsidiary of a large group is usually not an SME, however small it
is on its own.

### The tax definition is different

This catches people out. For the preferential corporate tax rates, LHDN uses
its own tests, not SME Corp's. The current rates for qualifying companies are
15% on the first RM150,000 of chargeable income, 17% on the next RM450,000 and
24% on the rest
([LHDN](https://www.hasil.gov.my/en/syarikat/kadar-cukai-syarikat/)).
To qualify, a company generally needs paid-up ordinary share capital of RM2.5
million or less and gross business income of RM50 million or less, plus
conditions on related companies and ownership
([LHDN SME page](https://www.hasil.gov.my/en/syarikat/sme/)).

So a services company with RM25 million in turnover can be outside SME Corp's
definition but still inside LHDN's. Check each one separately, and ask your
tax adviser about your own position.

## What might change, and what we don't know

The government hasn't published any proposed new thresholds. What's on the
record is the industry's wish list. The Federation of Malaysian Manufacturers
has repeatedly asked for the manufacturing threshold to go up to annual sales
of RM100 million and 300 full-time employees, arguing that the current limits
push mid-tier manufacturers into the "large" category too early
([Business Today](https://www.businesstoday.com.my/2026/10/08/govt-urged-to-revise-manufacturing-sme-threshold-to-rm100-million-300-workers/),
[Free Malaysia Today](https://www.freemalaysiatoday.com/category/nation/2026/01/06/revise-sme-threshold-upward-to-include-mid-tier-manufacturers-says-fmm)).

That's a proposal, not a decision. As of mid-November 2026, there's no
confirmed answer to:

- What the new turnover and headcount limits will be, for any sector
- Whether there will be a separate tier for growth-stage or mid-sized firms
- When a new definition takes effect, and whether existing schemes switch over
- Whether LHDN's tax tests will change alongside it

Treat anyone quoting "the new SME thresholds" with caution until KUSKOP or SME
Corp publishes them.

## Why it matters if you're growing

If you're comfortably micro or small, the review is unlikely to change much
for you in the short term. It matters most if you're close to a threshold,
growing fast, or part of a group.

- **Programme eligibility.** Many government SME programmes are tied to SME
  status, and each scheme sets its own conditions. Crossing a line can mean
  losing access, so check the terms before you plan around a scheme.
- **Lender assessment.** The World Bank report found lenders struggle to
  assess firms without conventional credit histories
  ([The Edge](https://theedgemalaysia.com/node/825379)). Clean, consistent
  numbers make you easier to lend to, whatever your category.
- **Operational strain.** The jump from 30 to 75 people, or from RM3 million
  to RM20 million in sales, is usually where spreadsheets, WhatsApp groups and
  manual stock counts stop coping.

## A checklist for growing SMEs

Use the review period to get your house in order, so you're ready whatever the
new definition says:

- Work out your current category on both SME Corp tests, using your latest
  audited turnover and actual full-time headcount
- Check your LHDN tax status separately, including paid-up capital and any
  related companies
- List the schemes, grants or financing you rely on and note which ones
  depend on SME status
- Get turnover, headcount and margins from one system rather than five
  spreadsheets, so you can answer eligibility questions in minutes
- Make sure your e-invoicing is settled; our
  [MyInvois guide for SMEs](/blog/myinvois-e-invoicing-guide-malaysian-smes)
  covers the options
- Identify the process that breaks first as you grow: orders, stock,
  invoicing or reporting
- Watch [SME Corp](https://smecorp.gov.my) and KUSKOP for the official
  announcement

## FAQ

### Is my business an SME under the current definition?

If you're in services and have RM20 million or less in annual sales, or 75 or
fewer full-time employees, you meet SME Corp's size test. For manufacturing,
the limits are RM50 million or 200 employees. You also need to pass the
ownership and listing exclusions.

### When will the new SME definition apply?

KUSKOP has said it aims to complete the review by 2027. No effective date or
new thresholds have been published yet.

### Will the thresholds go up?

Industry groups such as FMM have asked for higher limits for manufacturers,
but the government hasn't announced any figures. Plan on the current
definition until official numbers are out.

### Is the SME Corp definition the same as the tax definition?

No. LHDN's preferential rates use paid-up capital and gross income tests, so
a company can qualify under one and not the other.

